== Chapter VI

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The work of this enmity against God by way of opposition. First, it lusts. What the lusting of sin consists in. Its surprising of the soul. Its readiness to close with temptations. Its fighting and warring: (1.) in rebellion against the law of grace; (2.) in assaulting the soul.]
]

#smallcaps[We] have declared how this enmity works by way of #emph[aversion], and also the means the soul is to use to prevent its effects and its prevailing power. The second way in which it puts itself forth is #emph[opposition]. Enmity will #emph[oppose] and contend with whatever it is at enmity with. It is so in things natural and moral. As light and darkness, heat and cold, oppose each other, so do virtue and vice. So it is with sin and grace. These, says the apostle, are contrary to one another (Gal 5:17): ἀντίκειται ἀλλήλοις, they are “placed and set in mutual opposition,” and that continually and constantly, as we will see.

Now there are two ways in which #emph[enemies] carry on an opposition: first, by #emph[force]\; and secondly, by #emph[fraud] and deceit. So when the #emph[Egyptians] became enemies to the children of #emph[Israel] and carried on an enmity against them, #emph[Pharaoh] said (Exod 1:10), “let us deal shrewdly with them,” or rather, cunningly and subtly with this people. For so #emph[Stephen], with regard to this word, expresses it in Acts 7:19, by κατασοφισάμενος: he used “every kind of fraudulent sophistry.” And to this deceit they added #emph[force], in their grievous oppressions. This is the way and manner of things where there is a prevailing #emph[enmity]. And the #emph[law of sin] makes use of both in its enmity against God and our souls.

I will begin with the first: its actions #emph[as it were by way of force], in an open, outright opposition to God and his law, or to the #emph[good] that a believing soul wants to do in obedience to God and his law. And in this whole matter we must be careful to steer our course rightly, taking the #emph[Scripture] as our guide, with spiritual reason and experience as our companions. For there are many sandbanks in our course, which must be diligently avoided, so that none who consider these things are #emph[troubled] without cause or #emph[comforted] without a just foundation.

In this #emph[first way] in which this sin puts forth its #emph[enmity] in opposition, namely, #emph[as it were by force] or strength, there are four things, expressing so many distinct degrees in its progress and procedure in pursuing its enmity.

First, its general inclination: it #emph[lusts] (Gal 5:17).

Secondly, its particular way of contending: it #emph[fights or wars] (Rom 7:23; Jas 4:1; 1 Pet 2:11).

Thirdly, its success in this contest: #emph[it brings the soul into captivity to the law of sin] (Rom 7:23).

Fourthly, its growth and rage upon success: it rises to #emph[madness], as an enraged enemy will (Eccl 9:3). We must speak to all of these in order.

First, in general it is said to #emph[lust] (Gal 5:17): “The flesh lusts against the Spirit.” This word expresses the general nature of the opposition that the #emph[law of sin] makes against God and the rule of his Spirit or grace in those who believe, and so it expresses the least degree of that opposition. Whenever it does anything, it #emph[lusts]. Because #emph[burning] is the general action of fire, whatever else fire does, it also #emph[burns]. When fire does anything, it burns; and when the law of sin does anything, it #emph[lusts].

That is why all the actions of this law of sin are called the #emph[lusts of the flesh]. Gal 5:16: “you#super[+] will not gratify the lusts of the flesh.” Rom 13:14: “make no provision for the lusts of the flesh.” Nor are these #emph[lusts of the flesh] only those by which men act out their #emph[sensuality] in carousing, drunkenness, uncleanness, and the like. They include all the #emph[actions] of the law of sin whatever, in all the faculties and affections of the soul. So in Eph 2:3 we have mention of the #emph[desires], or #emph[wills], or #emph[lusts] of the mind, as well as of the flesh. The #emph[mind], the most spiritual part of the soul, has its #emph[lusts] no less than the #emph[sensual appetite], which at times seems more properly to be called the flesh. And in the products of these lusts there are defilements of the #emph[spirit] as well as of the #emph[flesh] (2 Cor 7:1), that is, of the #emph[mind] and #emph[understanding] as well as of the appetite and affections, and of the body that serves them. And our holiness consists in the #emph[blamelessness] of all these (1 Thess 5:23): “Now may the God of peace Himself sanctify you#super[+] completely, and may your#super[+] entire spirit, soul, and body be kept blameless at the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ.” Indeed, by the flesh in this matter the whole old man, or the law of sin, is #emph[meant] (John 3:6): “What is born of the flesh is flesh”; that is, it is all flesh, and nothing else. And whatever remains of the #emph[old] nature in the #emph[new man] is still flesh. And #emph[this flesh lusts]\; this law of sin does so, and this is the general basis and foundation of all its opposition to God. And it does this in two ways.

First, in a hidden, close #emph[inclination] to all evil. This lies in it #emph[habitually]. While a man is in the state of nature, fully under the power and dominion of this #emph[law of sin], it is said that “every figment of his heart is evil,” and that continually (Gen 6:5). It can frame, fashion, produce, or act nothing but what is evil, because this #emph[habitual inclination] to evil that is in the law of sin is absolutely predominant in such a man. It is in the heart like #emph[poison] that has nothing to lessen its venomous qualities, and so it infects whatever it touches. And where its power and dominion are broken, still in its own nature it has an #emph[habitual inclination] to what is evil, and its #emph[lusting] consists in this.

But here we must distinguish between #emph[the habitual frame of the heart and the natural inclination or habitual bent of the law of sin in the heart]. The #emph[habitual inclination of the heart] takes its name from the principle that bears the chief or sovereign rule in it. So in believers it is toward #emph[good], toward God, toward holiness, toward obedience. The heart is not #emph[habitually] inclined to evil by the remainders of indwelling sin. But this #emph[sin in the heart] has a constant #emph[habitual inclination] to evil in itself, in its own nature. This is what the apostle means by its #emph[being present with us]. It is present with me, that is, always, and for its own end, which is to #emph[lust] to sin.

It is with indwelling sin as with a #emph[river]. While its springs and fountains are open and water is continually supplied to its streams, set a dam before it, and the dam makes it rise and swell until it bears down everything or overflows the banks around it. Let these waters be #emph[lessened], dried up in good measure at their springs, and what remains may be held back and restrained. But as long as there is any #emph[running water], it will constantly press on whatever stands before it, according to its weight and strength, because it is its nature to do so. And if by any means it makes a passage, it will go on. So it is with #emph[indwelling sin]. While its springs and fountains are open, it is vain for men to set a #emph[dam] before it by their convictions, resolutions, vows and promises. They may check it for a while, but it will increase, rise high, and rage at one time or another, until it bears down all those convictions and resolutions, or makes itself an #emph[underground passage] by some secret lust that will give it full vent. But now suppose that its springs are much #emph[dried] up by regenerating grace, and its streams or actions lessened by holiness. Still, while anything of it remains, it will constantly be pressing to have vent, to press forward into actual sin. And this is its #emph[lusting].

And this #emph[habitual inclination] in it is shown in two ways.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, in its #emph[unexpected surprisings] of the soul into foolish, sinful figments and imaginations, which the soul did not look for and for which no occasion was given. It is with indwelling sin as it is with the contrary principle of sanctifying grace. Grace gives the soul, if I may say so, many a #emph[blessed surprise]. It often produces and brings forth a holy, spiritual frame in the heart and mind when we have had no previous reasoned considerations to work them to it. And this shows it to be a #emph[habitual principle] prevailing in the mind. So in Song 6:12: “Before I realized it, my soul made me like the chariots of Amminadib”; that is, free, willing and ready for communion with Christ. לא ידעתי, “I knew not”: it was done by the power of the Spirit of grace, so that I took no notice of it, as it were, until it was done. The frequent actings of grace in this way, stirring up acts of faith, love and delight in God, are evidences of its great strength and prevailing power in the soul. And so it is also with #emph[indwelling sin]. Before the #emph[soul is aware], without any provocation or temptation, when it does not know it, it is thrown into a vain and foolish frame. Sin produces its #emph[figments] secretly in the heart and gets ahead of the mind’s consideration of what it is doing. By this I mean those #emph[actus primo primi] [first motions], the first acts of the soul. They are #emph[involuntary] so far as they do not have the #emph[actual consent] of the will, but they are #emph[voluntary] so far as sin has its residence in the will. And unless the soul is awake to take speedy care to prevent where they tend, these #emph[surprisings] often set everything on fire, as it were, and draw the mind and affections into actual sin. For as by grace we are often, before we are aware, made like the “chariots of a willing people,” and are far engaged in heavenly-mindedness and communion with Christ, making speed in it as in a #emph[chariot], so by sin we are often carried, before we are aware, into disordered affections, foolish imaginations, and a pleasing delight in things that are neither good nor profitable. That is the reason for the apostle’s caution in Gal 6:1: ἐὰν προληφθῇ, if a man is #emph[surprised] unawares by a fault, or in a trespass. I do not doubt that the apostle takes into account there the subtlety of Satan and the power of temptation, which make him express a man’s falling into sin by προληφθῇ, “if he is surprised.” But this working of #emph[indwelling sin] is also taken into account in it, and that in the #emph[chief place], since without it nothing else could #emph[surprise] us. For without its help, whatever comes from outside, from Satan or the world, must have some #emph[parley] in the mind before it is received. But it is from within, from ourselves, that we are #emph[surprised]. By this we are thwarted and brought over to do what we do not want to do, and hindered from doing what we want to do.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[That is why, often, when the soul is doing something quite different, as it were, wholly engaged in another purpose, sin starts something in the heart or its imaginations that carries it away into what is evil and sinful. Indeed, to show its power, sometimes when the soul is seriously engaged in the #emph[mortification] of some sin, sin will by one means or another lead it away into a #emph[toying] with that very sin whose ruin it is seeking and whose mortification it is engaged in. But since there is in this working of the #emph[law of sin] a special #emph[enticing] or #emph[entangling], we will speak to it fully later. Now these #emph[surprisings] can come from nothing but a #emph[habitual inclination] to evil in the principle they come from: not a habitual inclination to actual sin in the mind or heart, but a habitual inclination to evil in the sin that is in the mind or heart. This gets ahead of the soul with its figments. Some may have observed how much communion with God is prevented by this, how many meditations are disturbed, how much the minds and consciences of men have been defiled by this action of sin. I know no greater burden in the life of a believer than these #emph[involuntary surprisings] of the soul: involuntary, I say, as to the actual consent of the will, but not so with respect to the corruption that is in the will and is their principle. And it is with respect to these that the apostle makes his complaint in Rom 7:24.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, this #emph[habitual inclination] shows itself in its #emph[readiness and promptness], without dispute or argument, to join and close with every temptation by which it may possibly be stirred up. We know it is the nature of fire to burn, because it immediately takes hold of whatever is #emph[combustible]. Let any temptation whatever be set before a man, one that is a temptation because its #emph[matter] suits his corruptions or because of the way it is put to him. Immediately he has to deal not only with the temptation as it is set before him from outside, but also with his own heart about it. Without further consideration or debate, the temptation has gained a friend in him. Not a moment passes between the #emph[proposal] and the necessity that lies on the soul to look to its enemy within. And this too shows a constant #emph[habitual inclination] to evil. Our Savior said of the assaults and temptations of Satan, “the prince of this world is coming, and he has nothing in Me” (John 14:30). He had more temptations from Satan and the world, both #emph[in intensity] and #emph[in extent], in number, quality and fierceness, than any of the sons of men ever had. Yet in all of them he had to deal only with what came from #emph[outside]. His holy heart had nothing that liked them, that suited them, or that was ready to receive them: “the prince of this world had nothing in him.” So it was with #emph[Adam]. When a temptation befell him, he had only its #emph[outward] proposal to look to; all was well within until the outward temptation took hold and prevailed. With us it is not so.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[In a #emph[city] that is united in itself, compact and whole, without divisions and parties, if an enemy approaches it, the rulers and inhabitants have no thought at all except how they may oppose the enemy #emph[outside] and resist his approaches. But if the city is #emph[divided] against itself, if there are factions and #emph[traitors] within, the very first thing they do is to look to the enemies at home, the traitors within. They must cut off the head of #emph[Sheba] if they want to be safe. All was well with #emph[Adam] within doors when Satan came, so that he had nothing to do but look to Satan’s assaults and approaches. But now, at the approach of any temptation, the soul must instantly look #emph[within], where it will find this #emph[traitor] at work, closing with the baits of Satan and stealing away the heart. And it always does this, which proves a #emph[habitual inclination]. In Ps 38:17 #emph[David] says, “I am ready to fall,” or “for falling”: כי אני לצלע נכון, “I am prepared” and disposed to #emph[slipping], to the sliding of my foot into sin (#emph[verse] 16), as he explains the meaning of that phrase in Ps 73:2, 3. From #emph[indwelling sin] there was a continual disposition in him to be #emph[slipping], stumbling, limping on every occasion or temptation. There is nothing so vain, foolish, ridiculous, or silly, nothing so vile and abominable, nothing so #emph[atheistic] or detestable, that if it is set before the soul by way of temptation, there is not something in this #emph[law of sin] ready to answer it before grace condemns it. And this is the first thing in the #emph[lusting] of the law of sin: it consists in its #emph[habitual inclination] to evil, which shows itself by the #emph[involuntary surprisings] of the soul into sin, and by its readiness to join, without dispute or consideration, with all temptations whatever.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, its #emph[lusting] consists in its #emph[actual] pressing after what is evil, and its #emph[actual] opposition to what is good. The former instances showed its constant readiness for this work; this now treats of the work itself. It is not only #emph[ready]\; for the most part it is always engaged. It #emph[lusts], says the Holy Spirit; it does so continually. It #emph[stirs] in the soul constantly by one act or another, almost as the spirits do in the blood, or the blood in the veins. The apostle calls this its tempting (Jas 1:14): “each one is tempted … by his own lust.” Now what is it to be tempted? It is to have something set before a man’s consideration which, if he closes with it, is evil; it is sin to him. This is sin’s trade: ἐπιθυμεῖ, “it lusts.” It is raising up in the heart, and setting before the mind and affections, what is evil, trying, as it were, whether the soul will close with its suggestions, or how far it can carry them on, even if it does not wholly prevail. Now when such a temptation comes from #emph[outside], it is to the soul an #emph[indifferent] thing, neither good nor evil, unless it is consented to. But the very proposal from #emph[within], being the soul’s own act, is its sin. And this is the work of the #emph[law of sin]. It is restlessly and continually raising up and setting before the soul countless different forms and appearances of evil, of this or that kind, indeed of every kind in which the nature of man is capable of exercising corruption. It hatches something or other, in matter, manner or circumstance, that is disordered, unspiritual, not answering to the rule, and sets it before the soul. And the apostle may have in view this power of sin to beget figments and ideas of actual evil in the heart in 1 Thess 5:22: ἀπὸ παντὸς εἴδους πονηροῦ ἀπέχεσθε, keep yourselves from every evil figment or #emph[idea] of sin in the heart. For the word used there nowhere means an #emph[outward form or appearance]\; it is not “the appearance of evil” but an #emph[evil idea] or figment that is meant. And this #emph[lusting of sin] is what the prophet describes in wicked men, in whom its law is predominant (Isa 57:20): “the wicked are like the storm-tossed sea, for it cannot be still, and its waves churn up mire and muck.” It is a picture that expresses most vividly the #emph[lustings] of the law of sin, restlessly and continually bubbling up in the heart with wicked, foolish and filthy imaginations and desires. This, then, is the first thing in the opposition that this enmity makes to God, namely, in its general inclination: it #emph[lusts].]

Secondly, there is its particular way of contending: it #emph[fights] or #emph[wars]. That is, it acts with strength and violence, as men do in war. First it #emph[lusts], stirring and moving disordered figments in the #emph[mind], and desires in the appetite and the #emph[affections], and setting them before the #emph[will]. But it does not rest there; it cannot rest. It urges, presses and pursues its proposals with earnestness, strength and vigor, fighting, contending and warring to obtain its end and purpose. If it merely #emph[stirred up] and #emph[proposed] things to the soul, and then immediately submitted to the sentence and judgment of the mind that the thing is evil, against God and his will, and not to be pressed any further, much sin that is now produced might be prevented. But it does not #emph[rest] here. It goes on to carry out its design, and that with earnestness and contention. By this means wicked men #emph[inflame themselves] (Isa 57:5). They are #emph[self-inflamers] to sin, as the word means. Every spark of sin is cherished in them until it grows into a flame; and so it will do in others where it is cherished in the same way.

Now this fighting or warring of sin consists in two things.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, in its #emph[rebellion] against grace, or the law of the mind.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, in its #emph[assaulting] the soul, contending for rule and sovereignty over it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The first is expressed by the apostle in Rom 7:23: “I find,” he says, “another law,” ἀντιστρατευόμενον τῷ νόμῳ τοῦ νοός μου, “rebelling against the law of my mind.” There are, it seems, #emph[two laws] in us: the law of the flesh, or of sin, and the law of the mind, or of grace. But contrary laws cannot both hold #emph[sovereign] power over the same person at the same time. In believers the sovereign power is in the hand of the law of grace. So the apostle declares in #emph[verse] 22: “For in my inner being I delight in God’s law.” Obedience to this law is performed with #emph[delight] and pleasure in the inner man, because its authority is #emph[lawful] and good. He says it more expressly in Rom 6:14: “For sin shall not have dominion over you#super[+], because you#super[+] are not under law, but under grace.” Now to war against the law that has a just sovereignty is to rebel. And that is what ἀντιστρατεύεσθαι means: it is to rebel, and it ought to have been translated so, “rebelling against the law of my mind.” And this rebellion consists in a stubborn, obstinate opposition to the commands and directions of the law of grace. Does the #emph[law of the mind] command anything as duty? Does it rise up severely against anything that is evil? When the lusting of the law of sin rises to this degree, it contends against obedience with all its might. The effect of this, as the apostle tells us, is “the doing of what we do not want to do, and the not doing of what we want to do” (#emph[verses] 15, 16). And from this place we may gather a notable instance of the power of sin in this rebellion of it. The #emph[law of grace] prevails on the will, so that it #emph[wants to do] what is good: “I have the desire to do what is good” (verse 18); “when I want to do good” (verse 19, and again verse 21); and it does not want to do “the evil I do not want to do” (verses 19, 20). And it prevails on the #emph[understanding], so that it approves or disapproves according to the dictates of the law of grace. Verse 16: “I admit that the law is good”; and #emph[verse] 15. The judgment always lies on the side of grace. It prevails also on the #emph[affections] (verse 22): “in my inner being I delight in God’s law.” Now if this is so, if grace has the sovereign power in the understanding, will, and affections, why does it not always prevail? Why do we not always do what we want to do, and abstain from what we do not want to do? Is it not strange that a man should not do what he chooses, wills, likes, and delights in? Is anything more required to enable us to do what is good? The law of grace does all that can be expected of it, all that is in itself abundantly sufficient for perfecting all holiness in the fear of the Lord. But here lies the difficulty: in the entangling opposition made by the #emph[rebellion] of this law of sin. Nor can it be expressed with what vigor and variety sin acts in this matter. At times it suggests distractions; at times it causes weariness; at times it finds out difficulties; at times it stirs up contrary affections; at times it breeds prejudices. One way or another it entangles the soul, so that it never allows grace to have an absolute and complete success in any duty. #emph[Verse] 18: τὸ κατεργάζεσθαι τὸ καλὸν οὐχ εὑρίσκω, I do not find the way #emph[perfectly to work] out or accomplish what is good, for that is what the word means; and that is because of this opposition and resistance made by the law of sin. Now this rebellion appears in two things.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, in the opposition it makes to the general #emph[purpose] and course of the soul.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, in the opposition it makes to #emph[particular] duties.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, in the opposition it makes to the #emph[general purpose] and course of the soul. There is no one in whom the Spirit of Christ is, who is his, whose general aim and purpose is not to walk in a universal conformity to him in all things. So it is with him, from the inward frame of the heart to the whole compass of his outward actions. God requires this in his covenant (Gen 17:1): “Walk before Me and be blameless.” Accordingly, his aim is to walk before God, and his frame is sincerity and uprightness in it. This is called “abiding in the Lord with purpose of heart” (Acts 11:23); that is, in all things. And it is not a lazy, dead, ineffectual purpose, but one that is at work and sets the whole soul to work in pursuing it. The apostle sets this out in Phil 3:12, 13, 14: “Not that I have already obtained all this, or have already been made perfect, but I press on to take hold of that for which Christ Jesus took hold of me. Brothers, I do not consider myself yet to have taken hold of it. But one thing I do: Forgetting what is behind and straining toward what is ahead, I press on toward the goal to win the prize of God’s heavenly calling in Christ Jesus.” He uses three words that excellently express the soul’s universal pursuit of this purpose of heart in clinging to God. First, he says, διώκω (verse 12), “I press on,” I pursue. The word properly means to #emph[persecute], and we know with what earnestness and diligence that is usually done. Secondly, ἐπεκτείνομαι, “straining toward,” reaching out with great intensity of spirit and affections; it is a great and constant effort that is expressed in that word. Thirdly, κατὰ σκοπὸν διώκω, “I press on toward the goal,” that is, just as men running for a prize. All these set out the vigor, earnestness, diligence and constancy used in pursuing this purpose. And the nature of the principle of grace requires this in those in whom it is. But still we see with what #emph[failings], indeed #emph[fallings], their pursuit of this course is attended. The frame of the heart is changed, the heart is stolen away, the affections are entangled, outbreaks of unbelief and disordered passions appear, and carnal wisdom with all its retinue is set to work, all contrary to the general principle and purpose of the soul. And all this comes from the #emph[rebellion] of this law of sin, stirring up and provoking the heart to disobedience. The prophet gives this #emph[character] of #emph[hypocrites] in Hos 10:2: “Their hearts are divided; now they will be found faulty.” Now though this is #emph[wholly] so, with respect to the mind and judgment, in hypocrites only, yet it is #emph[partly] so in the best, in the sense described. They have a division, not #emph[of] the heart, but #emph[in] the heart; and that is why they are so often found at fault. So the apostle says, “so that we cannot do what we want” (Gal 5:17). We cannot accomplish the aim of walking closely by the law of grace, because of the contrariness and rebellion of this law of sin.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it rebels also with respect to particular duties. It raises an uproar in the soul against the particular commands and designs of the law of grace. “You#super[+] cannot do what you#super[+] want”; that is, the duties you#super[+] judge to be required of you#super[+], which you#super[+] approve and delight in in the inner man, you#super[+] cannot do as you#super[+] want. Take prayer as an instance. A man sets himself to that duty. He wants not only to perform it, but to perform it in the manner the nature of the duty and his own condition require. He wants to “pray in the Spirit,” fervently, with sighs and groans too deep for words; in faith, with love and delight, pouring out his soul to the Lord. This is what he aims at. Now he will often find a rebellion in this matter, a fighting of the law of sin. He who thought to do #emph[all things] will find it hard to get #emph[anything] done. I do not say that it is #emph[always] so; but it is so when sin #emph[wars] and #emph[rebels], which is a special exercise of its power. Poor creatures often meet with woeful entanglements on this account. Instead of the free, enlarged communion with God that they aim at, the best their souls arrive at is to go away mourning for their folly, deadness and unfitness. In a word, there is no command of the law of grace that is known, liked, and approved by the soul, but when it comes to be obeyed, this #emph[law of sin] rises against it and rebels one way or another. And this is the first way of its #emph[fighting].]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it does not only #emph[rebel] and resist; it assaults the soul. It attacks the law of the mind and of grace, which is the second part of its warring. 1 Pet 2:11: στρατεύονται κατὰ τῆς ψυχῆς, “they fight,” or war, “against the soul.” Jas 4:1: στρατεύονται ἐν τοῖς μέλεσιν ὑμῶν, “they fight,” or war, “in your#super[+] members.” #emph[Peter] shows what they oppose and fight against, namely the #emph[soul] and the law of grace in it; #emph[James] shows what they fight with, or by, namely the members, or the corruption that is in our mortal bodies. ἀντιστρατεύεσθαι is to rebel against a superior; στρατεύεσθαι is to assault, or make war for superiority. Sin takes the part of an #emph[assailant] as well as of a resister. It makes attempts for rule and sovereignty, as well as opposing the rule of grace. Now all war and fighting has something of #emph[violence] in it, and so there is some #emph[violence] in that action of sin which the Scripture calls #emph[fighting] and warring. And this assailing efficacy of sin, as distinguished from its rebelling treated before, consists in the following things.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, all its #emph[positive] actions in stirring up to sin belong under this head. Often, by the vanity of the mind, or the sensuality of the affections, or the folly of the imaginations, it attacks the #emph[soul] at a time when the law of grace is not actually putting it to duty; so in this it does not rebel, but assault. That is why the apostle cries out in Rom 7:24, “Who will rescue me” from it? Who will #emph[rescue] me out of its hand, as the word means? When we #emph[pursue] an enemy and he resists us, we do not cry out, “Who will rescue us?” for we are the assailants. But “Who will rescue me?” is the cry of one who is attacked by an enemy. So it is here. A man is assaulted by his own #emph[lusts], as #emph[James] speaks. By the wayside, in his work, in the middle of a duty, sin attacks the soul with vain imaginations and foolish desires, and would gladly employ the soul in making provision for its satisfaction. The apostle warns us against this in Rom 13:14: τῆς σαρκὸς πρόνοιαν μὴ ποιεῖσθε εἰς ἐπιθυμίας, do not carry out the provision or the plans of the flesh for its own satisfaction.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, its #emph[persistence] and urgency seem to be indicated in this expression of its #emph[warring]. Enemies in war are restless, pressing and persistent. So is the law of sin. Does it attack the soul? Throw off its motions, and it comes back again. Rebuke them by the power of grace, and they withdraw for a while and then come back again. Set the cross of Christ before them, and they do as those did who came to arrest him: at the sight of him they drew back and fell to the ground, but they got up again and laid hands on him. Sin gives way for a time, but returns and presses on the soul again. Remind it of the love of God in Christ: though it is struck, it still does not give up. Set #emph[hell-fire] before it: it rushes into the middle of those flames. Reproach it with its folly and madness: it knows no shame, but still presses on. Let the thoughts of the mind try to flee from it: it follows as on the wings of the wind. And by this #emph[persistence] it wearies and wears out the soul; and if the great remedy of Rom 8:13 does not come in time, it prevails to a conquest. There is nothing more amazing or dreadful in the working of sin than this #emph[persistence] of it. The soul does not know what to make of it. It dislikes, abhors, and detests the evil sin tends to; it despises the thoughts of it and hates them as hell. And yet it has them forced on it by itself, as if it were another person, an outright enemy that had got inside him. The apostle lays all this open in Rom 7:15, 17: “what I hate, I do.” He is not speaking of outward actions, but of the inward risings of the mind. “I hate them,” he says; I detest them. But why, then, will he have anything more to do with them? If he hates them and abhors himself for them, let him leave them alone, have nothing more to do with them, and so end the matter. “Alas,” he says (#emph[verse] 17), “‘it is no longer I who do it, but it is sin dwelling in me that does it.’ I have one within me who is my enemy, who with endless, restless persistence forces these things on me, even the things that I hate and detest. I cannot be rid of them; I am weary of myself; I cannot flee from them. ‘What a wretched man I am! Who will rescue me?’” I do not say that this is the ordinary condition of believers, but it is often so when this law of sin rises up to war and fighting. It is not so with them with respect to particular sins, this or that sin, outward sins, sins of life or conduct. But with respect to vanity of mind and inward, spiritual disorders, it is often so. I know some claim great perfection, but I am resolved to believe the apostle before every one of them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, it carries on its #emph[war] by entangling the affections and drawing them into a league against the mind. Let grace be enthroned in the mind and judgment; yet if the #emph[law of sin] lays hold on and entangles the affections, or any of them, it has gained a fort from which it continually assaults the soul. That is why the great duty of #emph[mortification] is chiefly directed to take effect on the affections (Col 3:5): “Put to death, therefore, your#super[+] members that are on the earth: sexual immorality, impurity, lust, evil desires, and greed, which is idolatry.” The members that are on the earth are our affections. For sin is not seated in the outward part of the body; in particular, covetousness is not, and yet it is listed there to be mortified among our “members that are on the earth.” Indeed, after grace has taken possession of the soul, the #emph[affections] become the chief seat of the remainders of sin. And so #emph[Paul] says that this law “is in our members” (Rom 7:23), and #emph[James] that it wars in #emph[our members] (Jas 4:1); that is, in our affections. And there is no right estimate to be made of the work of #emph[mortification] except by the affections. Every day we may see persons of very eminent light who still visibly have unmortified hearts and lives. Their affections have not been #emph[crucified] with Christ. Now, then, when this law of sin can possess any affection whatever it is, love, delight, fear, it will make fearful assaults on the soul from it and by it. For instance, has it got someone’s love entangled with the world or the things of it, “the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, or the pride of life”? How it will take advantage of every occasion to break in on the soul! The soul will do nothing, attempt nothing, be in no place or company, perform no duty, private or public, but sin will have one blow or another at it; one way or another it will be pleading for itself.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This is the sum of what we will offer about this action of the #emph[law of sin] by way of #emph[fighting] and warring against our souls, which is so often mentioned in the Scripture. And a due consideration of it is of no small benefit to us, especially to bring us to self-abasement, to teach us to walk humbly and mournfully before God. There are two things suited to humble the souls of men: first, a due consideration of God, and then of themselves. Of God in his greatness, glory, holiness, power, majesty and authority; of ourselves in our lowly, abject, and sinful condition. Now of all things in our condition, nothing is so suited to this end and purpose as what lies before us: the vile remainders of enmity against God that are still in our hearts and natures. And it is no small evidence of a gracious soul when it is willing to search itself in this matter and to be helped in it by a word of truth, when it is willing that the word should dive into the secret parts of the heart and rip open whatever evil and corruption lies there. The prophet says of #emph[Ephraim] in Hos 10:11 that he “loves to thresh”: he loved to work when he could eat, to have the grain always before him. But God, he says, would make him #emph[plow], a labor no less needful, though not so pleasant at present. Most men love to hear of the #emph[doctrine of grace], of the pardon of sin, of free love, and suppose they find #emph[food] in it; at any rate it is evident that they grow and thrive in the life and notion of these things. But they do not delight so much in breaking up the #emph[fallow ground] of their hearts, in inquiring after the weeds and briars that grow in them, though this is no less necessary than the other. This path is not so beaten as that of grace, nor so much trodden, though it is the only way to come to a true knowledge of grace itself. Perhaps some who are wise and grown in other truths may still be so little skilled in searching their own hearts that they are slow in perceiving and understanding these things. But this #emph[sloth] and neglect must be shaken off if we have any regard for our own souls. It is more than probable that many a false #emph[hypocrite], who has deceived himself as well as others because he thought the doctrine of the gospel pleased him and so supposed he believed it, might be delivered from his soul-ruining deceits if he would diligently apply himself to this search of his own heart. Or would other #emph[professors] walk with as much boldness and security as some do, if they considered rightly what a deadly, watchful enemy they continually carry about with them and in them? Would they #emph[indulge] as much as they do in carnal joys and pleasures, or pursue their perishing affairs with as much delight and greed as they do? It is to be wished that we would all apply our hearts more to this work, to come to a true understanding of the nature, power and subtlety of this our adversary, so that our souls may be humbled; and that,]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, in walking with God. His delight is with the humble and contrite, those who tremble at his word, the mourners in #emph[Zion]\; and we are such only when we have a due sense of our own vile condition. This will produce reverence for God, a sense of our distance from him, wonder at his grace and condescension, and a due valuing of mercy, far above those light, wordy, airy #emph[attainments] that some have boasted of.]

Secondly, in walking with others. It lays in provision to prevent those great evils of judging, spiritual unmercifulness, and harsh censuring, which I have observed many to make a show of, men who at the same time, as afterward appeared, were guilty of greater or worse crimes than those they raved against in others. This, I say, will lead us to meekness, compassion, and readiness to forgive and to pass over offenses, when we consider what our own state is, as the #emph[apostle] plainly declares in Gal 6:1. The man who understands the evil of his own heart, how vile it is, is the only useful, fruitful, and solidly believing and obedient person. Others are fit only to delude themselves and to disturb families, churches, and all relationships whatever. Let us, then, consider our hearts wisely, and then go and see whether we can be proud of our #emph[gifts], our graces, our reputation and esteem among professors, our enjoyments. Let us go then and judge, condemn, and reproach others who have been tempted; we will find a great inconsistency in these things. And many things of the same kind might be added here from considering this woeful effect of #emph[indwelling sin]. The way of opposing and defeating its design in this will be considered later.
